,-704.7,510.7,-1060.3,512,-1067\nl0 -0\nc4.7,-7.3,11,-11,19,-11\nH40000v40H1012.3\ns-271.3,567,-271.3,567c-38.7,80.7,-84,175,-136,283c-52,108,-89.167,185.3,-111.5,232\nc-22.3,46.7,-33.8,70.3,-34.5,71c-4.7,4.7,-12.3,7,-23,7s-12,-1,-12,-1\ns-109,-253,-109,-253c-72.7,-168,-109.3,-252,-110,-252c-10.7,8,-22,16.7,-34,26\nc-22,17.3,-33.3,26,-34,26s-26,-26,-26,-26s76,-59,76,-59s76,-60,76,-60z\nM1001 80h400000v40h-400000z'/></svg></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.305em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"qd06ZfcVQV"}],"key":"cX4qJ96tVS"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"poL7P7Od3d"}],"key":"WxRTnhJIpj"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"Suppose a student answers 20 true/false questions completely at random. Use a normal approximation to estimate the probability of getting no more than 8 correct. The number (X) correct is a binomial random variable that represents the number of successes in 20 trials when the probability of success for each trial is .5. X has a mean and standard deviation of:\n\n$\\mu=np=20(0.5)=10,\\sigma=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}=\\sqrt{20(0.5)(1-0.5)}=2.24$\n\nand so we approximate the binomial X with a normal random variable having the same mean and standard deviation:\n\n```{figure} images/image182.gif\n:alt: A probability histogram with a probability distribution curve drawn on top. The vertical axis is labeled \"Probability\" and the horizontal axis is labeled \"X.\" The histogram is for binomial X, with mean=10 and standard deviation of 2.24 . The normal curve is for normal X, with mean 10 and standard deviation of 2.24 , the same as for the histogram.\n```\n\nThen we solve in the usual way using normal tables:\n\n$P(X_{B}\\leq8) \\approx P(X_{N}\\leq8)=P(Z\\leq\\frac{8-10}{2.24})=P(Z\\leq-0.89)=0.1867$","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"jBeva8Zun3"}],"key":"cWshMBph5T"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unfortunately, the approximated probability, .1867, is quite a bit different from the actual probability, .2517. However, this example constitutes something of a “worst-case scenario” according to the usual criteria for use of a normal approximation.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"ybwD46jCmL"}],"key":"vbJQ8v8rEd"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rule of Thumb","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"un3KcFDLzG"}],"identifier":"rule-of-thumb","label":"Rule of Thumb","html_id":"rule-of-thumb","implicit":true,"key":"yoMhvyevPy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Probabilities for a binomial random variable X with n and p may be approximated by those for a normal random variable having the same mean and standard deviation as long as the sample size n is large enough relative to the proportions of successes and failures, p and 1 - p. Our Rule of Thumb will be to require that","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"Fi9se4SHZ4"}],"key":"dihP5pT9Eg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"inlineMath","value":"np\\geq10","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">np\\geq10</anno